  <section id="film">
    <p class="section-label">The film</p>
    <h2>Do You Copy?</h2>
    <p class="dim">Pavel Balakin is a flight engineer aboard a failing Soviet space station, rationing power, food, and company as the ground below him goes quiet. The one voice that still gets through is Kat, back home. As his supplies run out and the silence stretches on, staying in contact with her becomes the only mission that still matters.</p>
    <p class="dim">Loosely inspired by the real cosmonauts left in orbit as the Soviet Union dissolved beneath them, cut off from a state that stopped answering, unsure what they'd be coming home to. "Do You Copy?" isn't a retelling of any one of their stories. It borrows the premise and asks a smaller, more personal question: what isolation does to the person still trying to reach you, and to the one who can't stop trying to answer.</p>
    <div class="status-row">
      <span class="badge">Draft 04</span>
      <span class="status-note">Script locked, written by Aidan Martin &amp; Phil Lemon</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="next">
    <p class="section-label">What's next</p>
    <h2>Speed and reach</h2>
    <p class="dim">Right now Call Sheet runs on one machine. The next four stretches take it from a tool that works for me to one another production could actually pick up.</p>
    <div class="roadmap">
      <div class="q">
        <span class="qnum">Q1</span>
        <h3>Harden &amp; validate</h3>
        <p>Stress-test the existing batch processor against real edge cases: mixed take types, retakes, partial batches, error recovery. Run a full time-saved pass against "Do You Copy?" takes, measured against the old manual workflow.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="q">
        <span class="qnum">Q2</span>
        <h3>Network job queue</h3>
        <p>Split shot and take processing across separate nodes on a render farm, so a big batch runs in parallel instead of grinding through one machine in sequence. Not about babysitting less, about real throughput.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="q">
        <span class="qnum">Q3</span>
        <h3>Standalone take manager</h3>
        <p>A take management interface that runs outside Unreal entirely, plus a general polish and hardening pass, the setup friction and edge cases that matter once it's not just running on my machine.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="q">
        <span class="qnum">Q4</span>
        <h3>Open source release</h3>
        <p>Ship Call Sheet v1, get it in front of a few other small or indie MetaHuman productions, and iterate on real feedback. "Do You Copy?" keeps using it as its own pipeline tool throughout.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

